    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0346" n="334" xml:id="pg334"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/16795: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Earle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-11-22T15:00:00+02:00">November 22,
                            1941—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>458. Legation’s 457, November 22.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d357-f1"/> The Prime
                    Minister’s speech, particularly his references to Bulgaria’s prevention of the
                    formation of a Balkan bloc, her fidelity to Germany and her attitude towards
                    communism, is interpreted here as being designed to make up for Bulgaria’s
                    failure to take positive action against Russia by emphasizing the contributions
                    her policy has made to the Axis cause. It is considered that Hitler’s reference
                    to Bulgaria in his speech of November 8 did not show complete approval of
                    Bulgaria’s attitude in the Russian conflict.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Earle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
